  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Alaska received $3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government awarded 8,176 contracts in Alaska, with an average award size of $366K. Obligations fell 22.3% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $1.1B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $554M and the General Services Administration with $492M. Davis Constructors & Engineers, Inc. is the top vendor with $470M in obligations, and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP follows with $261M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by spending at $946M, with Pharmacy Benefit Management and Other Third Party Administration of Insurance and Pension Funds next at $261M. Fiscal-year trends show variation: FY21 was $2.3B, FY22 was $3.3B, FY23 was $3.8B, FY24 was $3.8B, FY25 was $3.9B, and FY26 is $3B. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Alaska changed year over year?",
        "body": "Alaska obligations fell 22.3% year over year. Alaska total obligations for FY26 to date are $3B, down from $3.9B in FY25 and $3.8B in FY24 and FY23. Alaska spending rose from $2.3B in FY21 to $3.3B in FY22 before peaking in FY25. The current-year total is partial because the fiscal year is still in progress."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal spending in Alaska?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Alaska with $1.1B in obligations. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $554M and the General Services Administration obligated $492M. Davis Constructors & Engineers, Inc. is the top vendor with $470M, and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP is second with $261M."
      }
    ]
